\date{2025-11-09T21:56:05Z}
\title{Built-in commands}

\import{swarm-macros}

\p{Many built-in commands have simple typing rules like the following:

  \rules{
    \swty{\Gamma}{\sf{move}}{\Cmd{\Exc{\sf{move}}}{\Unit}}{\varnothing}
  }

  This says simply that \sf{move} is a command, and that executing it requires the corresponding capability. (Recall that writing a bare capability like #{\Exc{\sf{move}}} where a [requirement context](000Z) is expected [stands for the context with a singleton capability set and empty device set and inventory](001K).)  The #{\varnothing} indicates that no capabilities are required to \em{evaluate} \sf{move}; it is already a value so no evaluation is required.
}

\p{Some built-in commands take arguments, but they typically have a similar typing rule, with no capabilities on the arrows.  For example, no special capabilities are required to \em{evaluate} the application \app{\sf{turn}}{\sf{left}}.

\rules{
  \swty{\Gamma}{\sf{turn}}{\Fun{\Dir}{}{\Cmd{\Exc{\sf{turn}}}{\Unit}}}{\varnothing}
}

}
